  "worked_solution": "The sum and constant multiple rules for double summations state that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({a}_{ij}+{b}_{ij}) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{a}_{ij}+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{b}_{ij}, \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}k{a}_{ij} & =k{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{a}_{ij}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the sum rule to the given double summation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{16}({a}_{ij}+{b}_{ij}-2) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{16}{a}_{ij}+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{16}{b}_{ij}-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{16}2 \\\\  & =300+250-(2{\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{16}1) \\\\  & =550-2({\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{16}1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}1=mn,\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}550-2({\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{16}1) & =550-2\\cdot (15\\cdot 16) \\\\  & =550-480 \\\\  & =70.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{16}({a}_{ij}+{b}_{ij}-2)=70.\n$$",
